    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d14">

                <pb facs="0029" n="19" sameAs="frus:frus1945v03/pg19"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Austria)/2–945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi><anchor
                        n="58" xml:id="d14-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caserta</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-09T19:00:00+01:00">February 9, 1945—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:08 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>489. From Gray for Riddleberger<anchor n="59" xml:id="d14-f2"/> and Dunn. We
                    discussed with Mosely the matter of a formal communication to the Soviets (and
                    later to the French) telling them of the United States Austrian planning group
                    under General Flory and expressing the desire to establish contact with the
                    corresponding Soviet group at the earliest moment.</p>
                <p>In view of recent military developments and the fact that the Soviet control
                    group for Germany has been invited to London, it <pb facs="0030" n="20"
                        xml:id="pg20"/> might be well to suggest, after concerting with the British
                    authorities, that the United States group would be prepared to join with the
                    Soviet group at a place designated by the latter.</p>
                <p>I recommend that the Department take this up with the British and reach some
                    agreement as to the time and method of sending such a communication.</p>
                <p>This telegram has been cleared with Generals McNarney and Flory and Mosely.</p>
                <p>Repeated to London for the Ambassador and Mosely as number 65. [Gray.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d14-f1" n="58" type="source" xml:id="d14-f1-content">
                <p>Ambassador Kirk was also United States Representative on the Advisory Council for
                    Italy and was the United States Political Adviser on the Staff of the Supreme
                    Allied Commander, Mediterranean Theater (Alexander) whose headquarters were at
                    Caserta, Italy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d14-f2" n="59" xml:id="d14-f2-content">
                <p>James W. Riddleberger, Chief of the Division of Central European Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
